WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.002
-Michaela, thank you so much

00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:04.337
for coming on our show.
I appreciate it.

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:05.605
You're speaking to me
from London.

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:07.340
Is that --
Is that correct?

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.309
-Yes, East London, that's right.

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:12.211
-Oh. And how is --
How are you doing?

00:00:12.211 --> 00:00:13.746
How are you hanging in there?

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:17.217
-You know, it's kind of, like,
ups and downs.

00:00:17.217 --> 00:00:21.053
Sometimes I'm struggling
with the anxiety of sort of

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:23.923
not knowing what's going on,
like many other people.

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:30.496
So I'm sort of just occupying
myself with puzzles.

00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:32.832
I go running, but my knees
started getting sore,

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:36.836
so now I've been dancing in the
park, which has been good.

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:39.605
-Ooh, dancing in the park?

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:42.708
-I've been
dancing in the park, yes.

00:00:42.708 --> 00:00:45.978
Yeah. Basically I was running
a lot, like every day,

00:00:45.978 --> 00:00:47.914
but my knees
started getting sore.

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:50.083
But anyway, one of the times
when I was running,

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:53.553
I was going through my favorite
park, and I saw this guy.

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:56.556
And he was just sort of there,
stationary,

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:59.291
but doing these huge,
like, motions,

00:00:59.291 --> 00:01:01.427
dancing with
these huge ear phones on.

00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:04.631
And I thought it was so cool
that, as I was running by,

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:07.634
I just of gave him a thumbs-up
and I was giving him kisses.

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:10.069
And he sort of just, like,
gave me a thumbs-up back.

00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:12.005
But he was just doing his thing.

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:14.340
So then, my brother
came to stay with me, yeah.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.878
I was like, "Do you want
to dance in the park?"

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:21.447
And so we each
have our headphones on.

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:25.318
So he's got his own headphones,
I've got mine,

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:28.855
and we just go to the park,
and then we start just dancing.

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:31.123
And every now and again,
even though we're listening

00:01:31.123 --> 00:01:33.793
to separate music,
the beat, like, falls in...

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:36.228
-Oh, you hit the same groove,
and you're like...

00:01:36.228 --> 00:01:38.797
-Yeah, and then we start doing
the same, like, movements.

00:01:38.797 --> 00:01:41.401
I actually went myself today --
I went by myself.

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:45.004
-Did you get any thumbs up,
any kisses blown at you?

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:47.407
-I'm too nervous to look.
I just have to keep my head down

00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:49.108
and just sort of
just do my thing.

00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:51.644
-That's the way I dance
even at weddings.

00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:53.112
I just don't --

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:54.847
"No one look at
how bad this is."

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:57.183
-I don't even go to them
because my dancing's that bad.

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:58.785
-[ Laughing ]

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:01.520
Let's talk about
"I May Destroy You."

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.425
Wow! It's fresh, it's different,
it's so well done.

00:02:05.425 --> 00:02:06.793
Congratulations.

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:09.796
There was a great cover in
"New York Magazine" of you,

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:11.864
and, man, you look so cool.

00:02:11.864 --> 00:02:14.166
It was just, like,
such a stunning cover.

00:02:14.166 --> 00:02:15.168
Congrats on that.

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:16.703
You also wrote this whole thing.

00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:18.404
You wrote every episode
with no writers.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:19.806
You did.
You wrote everything.

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:21.540
-Yes.
-How are you doing this?

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:26.278
-I go away, and I just sort of
type on the keypad.

00:02:26.278 --> 00:02:27.813
I don't plan.

00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:29.181
Like, I had loads of drafts --

00:02:29.181 --> 00:02:32.252
I think like 20 drafts
of each episode.

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:35.455
I just kind of type
until it makes sense.

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:39.158
And I have a very dedicated team
of exec producers

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:40.860
and a great script editor.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.328
You know, my first two drafts,

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:45.265
I don't think
they made any sense,

00:02:45.265 --> 00:02:47.833
grammatically, editorially.

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:52.004
I think it just looks like
goo-goo-gah-gah-goo.

00:02:52.004 --> 00:02:53.539
And they just sort of take it
and go,

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:55.074
"Wow.
This is really interesting.

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:56.943
What does this mean?
What does this mean?"

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:58.578
I go, "It means this,"
and they're like, "Okay.

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:00.913
Why don't you go back
and try again

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:02.848
to write down
what you just said."

00:03:02.848 --> 00:03:05.118
So then I keep doing that
for 2 1/2 years,

00:03:05.118 --> 00:03:07.119
and then we have the show.
-Wow.

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:10.589
It's semi-autobiographical,
if I'm correct?

00:03:10.589 --> 00:03:11.724
Do you want to set up the show

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:13.526
for anyone
that doesn't know what it is?

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:16.095
-Yeah, I can try
to set up the show.

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:19.098
It is semi-autobiographical.

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:22.969
I based the character
of Arabella on myself,

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:24.603
especially in the beginning.

00:03:24.603 --> 00:03:27.440
She's a writer.
She takes a break from work.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.776
She goes out for a drink,
and her drink is spiked,

00:03:29.776 --> 00:03:31.744
and she's sexually assaulted
by strangers.

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:33.545
This was also my story.

00:03:33.545 --> 00:03:37.049
So that was the initial
inspiration with that character.

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:39.786
But then I asked
many more people questions

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:42.655
and realized that so many people

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:45.691
have a story
around sexual consent.

00:03:45.691 --> 00:03:48.928
So I broadened out,
and I kind of have these

00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:53.699
three essential characters,
Kwame, Arabella, and Terry.

00:03:53.699 --> 00:03:55.201
How can I sum it up?

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:57.569
They're navigating sexual life

00:03:57.569 --> 00:03:59.939
and the line between liberation
and exploitation.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:03.276
But it's also about loads more.
It's also just about friendship

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:07.046
and about how we think
and relate to one another today

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:10.282
and how much introspection
we really have.

00:04:10.282 --> 00:04:12.951
-I was impressed
the way you talk about

00:04:12.951 --> 00:04:15.355
and address these issues,

00:04:15.355 --> 00:04:19.225
but also you balance comedy
with seriousness.

00:04:19.225 --> 00:04:21.861
'Cause I will let everyone know,
it is funny, too.

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:24.163
It's so different and well done.

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:28.935
Do you think about, like,
"This thing needs a joke" or...?

00:04:28.935 --> 00:04:32.205
-No, no, I don't.
I don't ever do that.

00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:35.742
I just write,
and it comes out like that.

00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:40.112
I don't quite know yet
the method to

00:04:40.112 --> 00:04:42.715
the way it comes out.
But I do often go back --

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:46.619
When I try and figure out
how the tone emerged as it was,

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:48.721
I think back to my own reality

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:52.091
when I was
in the police investigation room

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:54.693
getting ready to give
my first witness statement.

00:04:54.693 --> 00:04:58.798
And I sat there sort of
realizing the course of my life

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:00.199
was forever going to change.

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:02.902
And I was sort of realizing
something bad had happened.

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:04.904
And my friend
was taking care of me,

00:05:04.904 --> 00:05:06.738
and as I looked to him,

00:05:06.738 --> 00:05:09.408
I noticed he was playing
"Pokémon Go" on his phone.

00:05:09.408 --> 00:05:13.045
And that just -- just --
just was like...

00:05:13.045 --> 00:05:16.649
-Oh.
That's real. That's real.

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:17.783
-Yeah.
-Like, "Oh, my --

00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:20.086
What are you seriously doing?"

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:21.854
Oh, wow.
-It was kind of like,

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:23.856
"Where am I?
What is everything?"

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:27.093
-Yeah, "What is everything?
Is life real?

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:28.894
"What just --"
Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:28.894 --> 00:05:31.164
-"What a circus.
What's going on?

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:33.666
It's rape and "Pokémon Go,"

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:36.535
and I don't know what to do
with those two things.

00:05:36.535 --> 00:05:39.305
But here they are, in one room.
-Yeah.

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:43.576
And you made it into a show
and into art.

00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:45.578
And it's great.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:05:45.578 --> 00:05:48.014
Here's Michaela Coel in
"I May Destroy You."

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:49.148
Take a look.

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:52.251
-This is the same guy, yeah?

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:54.086
That told you
you had a drug problem

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:58.858
and then sold you drugs
in the same breath.

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:01.226
-It wasn't in the same breath.
-What?

00:06:01.226 --> 00:06:02.528
-It was in the same night,
which is different.

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.896
-Listen, he's a hypocrite.

00:06:04.896 --> 00:06:07.166
He's controlling.
Is being around

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:08.901
a controlling hypocrite
good for your recovery?

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:11.069
Let me just check
the recovery toolbox.

00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:14.607
"Ooh! Surround yourself
with people who affirm you."

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:18.877
-And that's you?
-Yes.

00:06:18.877 --> 00:06:20.346
-That's you?
-Yes, it is.

00:06:20.346 --> 00:06:22.648
-So if you were to suddenly
not affirm me

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:24.983
when I needed affirmation
the most, it would make you

00:06:24.983 --> 00:06:27.486
someone I should stay away from,
I bet.

00:06:27.486 --> 00:06:30.123
Controlling and hypocritical?

00:06:30.123 --> 00:06:31.724
-My thanks to Michaela Coel.

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:35.695
"I May Destroy You" airs Mondays
at 9:00 p.m., on HBO.

00:06:35.695 --> 00:06:36.796
You are awesome.

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:38.563
Thank you so much
for coming on my show.

00:06:38.563 --> 00:06:39.732
-Thanks for having me.

